WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.380
I do not mean to be remotely competitive,
but I would point out that my report is even

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.860
shorter. That probably has more to do with
the fact that at this stage of the year there

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:28.330
is relatively little to report, than any great
ability on my part to write succinct reports,

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:32.570
so I cannot promise to keep them quite as
short as the year goes by.

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:38.980
Just four things to pull out of the report
as it stands. The first is just to pick up

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.280
on where Simon was earlier on, just to report
that our Annual Report and Accounts were laid

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.469
in Parliament this week. For those of you
who have not yet read them, I have a few copies

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:51.309
available for a small price. They make really,
really good holiday reading, so do apply to

00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:56.410
me afterwards. Of course, the good news, having
got 2014/2015 out of the way, is that I can

00:00:56.410 --> 00:01:01.280
now join the rest of you in 2015/2016, which
will be very exciting for me. That is the

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.780
first point.
The second one is to confirm what I indicated

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.869
when we met back in whenever it was, May I
suppose, that we have now agreed balance plans

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:16.929
with the CCGs and with our own direct commissioning
teams. It is set out in the report. It is

00:01:16.929 --> 00:01:21.099
important to point out that in getting to
those balance plans we have had to utilise

00:01:21.099 --> 00:01:27.240
£579 million of our historical surpluses
carried forward, of the total of just over

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:33.020
£1 billion that is available to us over time;
the obvious point being that that is a limited

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:38.849
resource which we are using up at a reasonable
rate. That gives us the total resource of

00:01:38.849 --> 00:01:44.750
just over £101 billion that you can see set
out in the report for this year.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:49.099
These plans, as Barbara said, have been calibrated
to provide activity growth in line with our

00:01:49.099 --> 00:01:53.780
modelling of what we think is required to
deliver all those core constitutional standards,

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:59.119
and what we are now doing is working with
colleagues in the TDA and with Monitor to

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:03.739
ensure that all of that activity is reflected
in the provider plans that Simon talked about

00:02:03.739 --> 00:02:08.950
earlier on, that the cost containment measures
which have been implemented have their full

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:14.520
impact on the financial plans, in particular
of providers. Of course, that is important

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:21.540
because the main submissions from providers
showed a slightly over £2 billion deficit

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:28.670
in that sector, which compares with a roundabout
£1 billion overspend in 2014/2015, and it

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:32.770
is really important that we get that level
of overspend back at the very worst to the

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:38.240
same as it was in 2014/2015, if not better
than that, because there really is not the

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:45.740
non-recurrent resource anywhere to cope with
a bigger level of deficit in that sector than

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.310
there was last year. So it is a big undertaking
that we are fully engaged with, and I would

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:55.460
want to reassure the Board that we are playing
our full part in making all of that possible,

00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:00.010
challenging as it is.
Thirdly, having got to balance plans, which

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:06.010
is great, there is the small matter of delivering
against them. The right-hand side of my report

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:11.780
sets out the financial position as at month
two. As is usual at this stage of the year,

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:16.050
I am struggling for newsworthy things to say
because there is not a great deal of movement

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:20.560
in the forecast, there is not a great deal
of information to base one’s insight on.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.930
The only significant factor that is pulled
out in the numbers there links back to the

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:29.870
earlier item that Bruce presented; there is
an overspend already in 2015/2016, predictable

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:36.740
of £70 million against the £340 million
budget that you talked about. There is a limited

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:40.780
amount that we can do about that in year in
2015/2016, but it just underlines again the

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:46.340
points that you made very eloquently, that
we need to find a better means of controlling

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.959
that going forward, for financial as well
as other reasons.

00:03:50.959 --> 00:03:57.030
Of itself, the 0.1% forecast overspend you
can see at the top of page three is within

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:02.130
normal tolerances at this stage of the year,
but I do also need to draw your attention

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:08.360
to the not so small print at paragraph 3.4,
which highlights the risk profile against

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:13.780
that ostensibly largely balanced position.
As you know, we have been reviewing that with

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:19.519
the relevant board committees on a regular
basis, but when we look at the net risk profile

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:24.750
that comes up from CCGs and direct commissioning
units, and when we superimpose a few national

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:30.120
issues that we talk about, without mitigation
that would significantly increase the level

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:36.240
of overspend that is in the forecast. I would
want to reassure the Board that to a large

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.780
extent we have mitigations identified for
the bulk of those risks, but I think what

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:47.810
it just underlines is that there is a very,
very tight balance of risk in our position,

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:54.210
and we are going to have to keep, as a system,
a very, very close check on the emergence

00:04:54.210 --> 00:05:00.630
of those risks and to be very swift with our
mitigation strategies. The executive group

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:04.040
will be spending a lot of time on that in
September, because that is the time when I

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:08.660
think the intelligence comes together with
some of the actions that we may need to take

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:13.720
to correct any adverse trends.
It also highlights finally the point I make

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:20.110
towards the end of the report about the critical
need to avoid unexpected failures in the system.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:24.930
You will be aware that last year we had a
small handful of individual organisations

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:31.210
who, as the year went by, frankly disappointed
in terms of the financial delivery, for a

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:36.900
variety of reasons. We have analysed all of
those reasons in depth, we have shared that

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:42.740
insight both with the working group we have
with the CCGs, and indeed with our own board

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.510
committees; in particular, the Investment
Committee has taken a strong interest in it.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:50.720
There are some quite discernible patterns
as to the causation of those failures, which

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:56.040
we have transcribed into a pretty comprehensive
toolkit that is being put together and a set

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:01.139
of processes that we are now rolling out,
and working with CCGs in particular to deploy,

00:06:01.139 --> 00:06:05.840
so that the lessons we have learnt about why
those particular places went wrong, why it

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.229
took us longer to detect them than would have
been ideal, and why, when we then started

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:14.139
to intervene, it has taken a long time to
get a grip on it. Those lessons we are trying

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:18.949
to learn in advance of the next few that would
be susceptible to that, and there is action

00:06:18.949 --> 00:06:26.740
going on right now with CCGs to look proactively
at what strengthening of governance, controls,

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:31.639
financial management, etc. we need to put
in place with them, but predominantly for

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:38.850
them to put in place through their own governance
to prevent rather than deal with financial

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:43.300
failure at a time when we simply will not
be able to compensate for that if it should

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:48.830
occur. I just wanted to draw your attention
to that. That is about it.

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:49.510
Thank you, Paul, and thank you for that assurance.

